    description: "A historic church and major pilgrimage site, home to life-sized statues of the Last Supper.",
    overview: "The National Shrine of Saint Joseph, also known as the Mandaue Church, is a historic stone church located in the city center of Mandaue. Established by the Jesuits in the early 17th century, the current structure has undergone several renovations. The church is famous for its unique set of life-sized ivory and wooden statues depicting the Last Supper, which are paraded during the Lenten season.\n\nVisitors will appreciate the church's solemn atmosphere and its beautiful facade. The interior is spacious and traditional, featuring a grand main altar. The shrine is a popular destination for pilgrims and tourists alike, especially during its annual fiesta and the Holy Week. It is a cornerstone of Mandaue's identity and a primary center for the city's religious activities.",
    significance: "This church is significant as one of the oldest parishes in the region, established even before Mandaue became a city. It represents the early missionary efforts to Christianize the agricultural and merchant communities of the area. The shrine status highlights its national importance as a site of devotion to Saint Joseph.\n\nCulturally, the church is the heart of Mandaue's most important traditions. The statues of the Last Supper are a unique artistic and religious treasure that sets this church apart. Its survival through various historical periods, including the colonial and wartime eras, makes it a resilient symbol of the Mandauehanon's faith and heritage.",
